    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A method to predict two- and three-dimensional subsurface joint networks from one-dimensional (1D bore hole image/core data is presented. Joint spatial density, connectivity, and prediction uncertainty are calculated in rocks around a borehole. The technique is applicable to inclined and horizontal boreholes in both layered and massive rocks. By combining the borehole observations and joint mechanics knowledge from outcrop and laboratory results, a first order reconstruction of the geometry of the subsurface joint network can be obtained. Such reconstructed joint network geometries are an essential starting point for many petroleum and hydro-geological applications such as perforation strategy, well productivity and cumulative well production.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
